(Software Engineering: REST APIs and Authentication) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A REST API exposes resources through HTTP. A resource is identified by a URI, and) Tj T*
(representations are exchanged using request and response messages. A client sends a request) Tj T*
(to a server; the server validates and processes it, then returns a status code and, when) Tj T*
(appropriate, a representation such as JSON. REST is an architectural style, not a particular) Tj T*
(programming language or vendor product.) Tj T*
(HTTP methods communicate the requested operation. GET retrieves a representation and should) Tj T*
(not change server state. POST submits data or requests creation of a subordinate resource.) Tj T*
(PUT replaces a resource representation and is ordinarily idempotent: repeating the same) Tj T*
(request has the same intended effect. PATCH applies a partial update. DELETE requests) Tj T*
(removal. Servers should document method behavior and validate request bodies before changing) Tj T*
(data.) Tj T*
(HTTP status codes report the outcome. The 2xx class indicates success: 200 means a request) Tj T*
(succeeded, 201 means a resource was created, and 204 means success with no response body.) Tj T*
(The 4xx class indicates a client-side problem: 400 is a malformed or invalid request, 401) Tj T*
(means valid authentication credentials are missing or unacceptable, 403 means the caller is) Tj T*
(authenticated but lacks permission, and 404 means the resource was not found. The 5xx class) Tj T*
(indicates that the server failed to fulfill an otherwise valid request. A status code alone) Tj T*
(does not explain every business rule, so APIs commonly return a structured error body too.) Tj T*
(Authentication establishes who a caller is; authorization decides what that caller may do. A) Tj T*
(JWT access token is a signed set of claims that a server can validate. A typical API) Tj T*
(verifies its signature, accepted algorithm, expiry, and subject before loading the) Tj T*
(corresponding account. The subject identifies the authenticated principal; a client-supplied) Tj T*
(user identifier is not proof of identity. Authorization must still check ownership or) Tj T*
(permissions for each requested resource. Possessing a valid token does not grant access to) Tj T*
(every document.) Tj T*
(A typical API request and response lifecycle begins when a client sends an HTTP method, URI,) Tj T*
(headers, and optional body. A router matches the URI and method. Authentication middleware) Tj T*
(or a dependency validates credentials and establishes the principal. Authorization checks) Tj T*
(that principal against the target resource. Input validation checks types and constraints.) Tj T*
(The application executes its service and database work, commits successful changes, and) Tj T*
(serializes a response with an appropriate status code and headers. Errors should be mapped) Tj T*
(to clear client responses while sensitive internal details stay in server logs.) Tj T*
(For a protected document question endpoint, the authenticated account should be derived from) Tj T*
(the verified token. The service should first load the conversation and verify its owner,) Tj T*
(then resolve the conversation's configured document scope. Only after these checks should it) Tj T*
(query document evidence. This ordering makes identity and authorization independent of the) Tj T*
(wording of the question and prevents a request from selecting another account's documents by) Tj T*
(changing a JSON field.) Tj T*
